1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,361
Here's how to reverse engineer Statuspage from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,361 --> 00:00:07,200
Claude passed all eighteen checks, and found fifty-eight gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:10,034
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,311 --> 00:00:13,682
It copies the part of Atlassian Statuspage a small software team needs.

5
00:00:13,938 --> 00:00:16,007
Each part of the product gets a live status.

6
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:17,500
Incidents get timed updates.

7
00:00:17,692 --> 00:00:19,544
Maintenance starts and ends on time.

8
00:00:19,732 --> 00:00:23,238
And customers subscribe by email to only the parts they use.

9
00:00:24,317 --> 00:00:26,002
The core rule is uptime.

10
00:00:26,194 --> 00:00:29,095
Every status change is written to a log that's never edited.

11
00:00:29,351 --> 00:00:34,386
Uptime over ninety days counts every minute of major outage, and half of every minute of partial outage.

12
00:00:34,599 --> 00:00:36,322
Degraded and maintenance count as up.

13
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:41,711
So forty-two minutes of API outage shows as ninety-nine point nine six percent, rounded down.

14
00:00:42,817 --> 00:00:45,419
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:45,675 --> 00:00:47,361
The foundation and the public page.

16
00:00:47,595 --> 00:00:48,854
Incidents and maintenance.

17
00:00:49,046 --> 00:00:52,033
Subscribers, emails, the feed and the API.

18
00:00:52,289 --> 00:00:53,906
Then automation and postmortems.

19
00:00:54,116 --> 00:00:58,597
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

20
00:00:59,683 --> 00:01:00,622
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:06,040
The public page shows each component with ninety daily bars, and one red day for the API.

22
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:09,260
The incident page lists every update, newest first.

23
00:01:09,492 --> 00:01:13,140
Owen starts a new incident from a template, with a preview of the subscriber email.

24
00:01:13,418 --> 00:01:15,487
His dashboard now shows a partial outage.

25
00:01:15,807 --> 00:01:19,419
And the outbox holds the email, with its one-click unsubscribe link.

26
00:01:20,517 --> 00:01:26,966
In one final run, on a fresh database, all eighteen checks passed, along with one hundred and fourteen automated tests.

27
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:30,034
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:30,312 --> 00:01:33,426
The test build exposed fifty-eight places where Claude had to guess.

29
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,242
A hidden component could still set the banner for the whole page.

30
00:01:36,454 --> 00:01:40,017
Caching a subscriber's own page could show their email address to the next visitor.

31
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:44,198
And one check said C D T, which was wrong once daylight saving ended.

32
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:46,296
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

33
00:01:47,383 --> 00:01:49,751
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

34
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:58,090
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the every-minute job on a real scheduler, a real monitoring tool, or hosting it apart from your product.

35
00:01:58,345 --> 00:02:00,818
Those are yours to check before customers rely on it.

36
00:02:01,883 --> 00:02:03,397
So is it worth building?

37
00:02:03,675 --> 00:02:07,455
Statuspage is free for one hundred subscribers and two team members.

38
00:02:07,694 --> 00:02:16,049
Paid plans run from twenty-nine to fourteen hundred and ninety-nine dollars a month, and subscribing to single components starts on Business, at three hundred and ninety-nine.

39
00:02:16,298 --> 00:02:19,882
If you need text messages, Slack alerts or private pages, buy it.

40
00:02:20,181 --> 00:02:23,032
If you want component subscriptions on your own domain, build it.

41
00:02:24,117 --> 00:02:25,461
Everything is in one file.

42
00:02:25,696 --> 00:02:33,100
The data model, twelve rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all eighteen checks, with the fifty-eight fixes already in.

43
00:02:33,967 --> 00:02:37,458
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

44
00:02:37,458 --> 00:02:39,588
If you build it, tell me what broke.

45
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:40,061
I'm Sam.

46
00:02:40,061 --> 00:02:41,067
See you tomorrow.

